A||||12||Gokart||The RIGHT battery||||5||
Z||000000||Gokart||04-27-2000||06:34 AM||||When Cobrah was over my place a while back he noticed the battery I have wasn't really the right one for a Fiero. The terminals are on the side but it won't stay in place cause the plastic hold down doesn't meet with the plastic lip on the battery. This won't be an issue soon when I move the battery up front, but I still want the RIGHT battery for a Fiero. Anyone know?||||reg||5
Z||000001||Frank R||04-27-2000||11:07 AM||||The "right" battery has no answer.  The right battery is one that will do the assigned task.  Batteries come in a waide variety of capacity and shapes.  The supplier will be in the best position to give application advice.  In many applications, dual battery connections are very handy for amps and other hi energy use.  The big boxes always have the best prices and shapes available.  Most battery failure is contributed to poor termination  [connection] or poor maintence,  failure to monitor water level.  Clean tight connection are paramount.  Frank R.||||reg||6
Z||000002||1FST2M6||04-27-2000||11:26 AM||||he's right there is no "Right bettery" but what you need to upgrade to is a NAPA Orbital! It's a Dry Cell. WAY better tan the over priced Optima... (anyone wanna buy an optima?) I've got one for sale...  the dry cell will NOT leak! and is $30 cheaper than my Optima.. i'm not bitter, just broke. ||||reg||1
Z||000003||Carrolles||04-27-2000||12:49 PM||||I like the one I got from WalMart.  I don't remember the size but it fits perfectly.  It also has both side and top terminals.  This is good because I don't have trouble trying to connect jumper cables to the side terminals.  The side terminals power the car.  I leave the plastic caps on the top terminals when they are not in use.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Frank R||04-27-2000||01:01 PM||||Repost on batterys.  The best battery available is one of the gel cell variety.  These batteries are used in the aviation industry.  They cannot leak and can be mounted in any position.  The optima is a very high quality design and other than being a little spendy, if you have the space and money  "go for it".  This type battery will withstand lots of abuse and keep on ticking.  A good battery's voltage will reflect 12.6 volts measured with a digital voltmeter.  A 75% discharged battery will be 12.0 volts.  Clean and tite...Clean and tite  Frank R.||||reg||6
Z||000005||cobra427||04-27-2000||09:56 PM||||Just to clarfy what I said as to the "right" battery, I was referring to the physical demensions of the battery. One that would fit in the location that it was designed for and be held down with the retaining block and bolt at the base of the battery.<P>As far as thos Napa dry cell batterys go,if you don't mind spending $147.00 for a battery,go for it. Personally, I think it is a little overkill. Thos batteries were designed for rough use/heavy vibration applications. (Marine/offroad)<P>If i hadn't bought my DieHard about a year ago,I probably would explore the new DieHard Security battery.On paper it looks very intriging. In reality. can't say.||||reg||1
Z||000006||cobra427||04-27-2000||09:57 PM||||Just to clarfy what I said as to the "right" battery, I was referring to the physical demensions of the battery. One that would fit in the location that it was designed for and be held down with the retaining block and bolt at the base of the battery.<P>As far as thos Napa dry cell batterys go,if you don't mind spending $147.00 for a battery,go for it. Personally, I think it is a little overkill. Thos batteries were designed for rough use/heavy vibration applications. (Marine/offroad)<P>If i hadn't bought my DieHard about a year ago,(72 mo.)I probably would explore the new DieHard Security battery.On paper it looks very intriging. In reality. can't say.<P>cobra427||||reg||1
Z||000007||Phil||04-27-2000||10:08 PM||||Just go to wally world and use their little battery selector - has the Fiero listed||||reg||1
Z||000008||Frank R||04-27-2000||11:18 PM||||A correct physical size battery for a Fiero should cost about $38-44, from Costco, Walmart,Kragen, Pep Boys etc with about 6 years warranty and free alternator check.<BR>Frank R.||||reg||6
Z||000009||RFMike||04-28-2000||12:42 AM||||ScatterGun comments.<P>The "correct" battery is one sized for your car's load requirements and alternator capacity.  If you have the high output alternator (94 Amp?- somebody check me), get a high capacity battery, in the 600-800 CCA range.  This is actually more capacity than you really need, but is nice to have if you live in a cold climate, and drive in winter.  I think a 600 CCA battery was about as large as you would get in 84.   <BR>If you don't have ac, electric windows, rear defrost, etc.  (electrical load elements), you may have the lower output alternator.  If you get a high capacity battery (800-1000 CCA), and you drive a lot of short hops, with a lot of restarts, you may not be able to charge the battery, and consequently not get the expected life from it.  A rare case of less is better.<BR>As far as physical size, if you are creating a custom location, it's whatever fits, with the right output.<BR>There are only three or four battery manufacturers, selling to distributors.  Gould, Champion, can't think of the others.  Way past my bedtime.<BR>Mike||||reg||1
Z||000010||Standard||04-28-2000||12:48 AM||||Yeah, I'm pretty sure the standard h/o alternator is 94 amps.  Theirs also a 100 amp alternator listed in the RPO sheets I have, also an 85 and a 66 amp.    <P>K22 Generator, 94 ampere<BR>K60 Generator, 100 ampere<BR>K81 Generator, 66 ampere<BR>K99 Generator, 85 ampere||||reg||1
Z||000011||Frank R||04-30-2000||12:41 PM||||There is NO relationship between battery ampacity [read capacity] and the output from an alternator.  The high numbers of an alternator only indicates how much amps [read watts] that the unit can produce.  Hi out put alternators just recharge a battery quicker than a lower out put unit.  The secret to haveing a good electrical system is clean and tight, especially the ground connections.  The battery cables cannot be tooo large.<BR>Frank R||||reg||6
Z||000012||Standard||05-01-2000||02:32 AM||||clean and tight makes a lot of things better.. hehe  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">  &lt;running for cover&gt;||||reg||10
